To create a new line within a cell in LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. Here is an example:

```latex
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
```

However, if you want to keep the `\\` command within a cell, without splitting it across multiple cells, you can use the `array` package and its `m{}` column type, which allows for line breaks within a cell. Here is an example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{array}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|m{5cm}|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `p{}` column type from the `array` package, but it requires a fixed height for the cell, which might not be what you want.

Another option is to use the `longtable` package, which allows for line breaks within a cell and also provides a way to continue a table over multiple pages.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{longtable}

\begin{document}

\begin{longtable}{|p{5cm}|}
\hline
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\hline
\end{longtable}

\end{document}
```

Note that you can adjust the `5cm` value to suit your needs.

If you want to have more control over the table and its alignment, you can use the `booktabs` package, which provides a more professional-looking table layout. However, it does not provide a built-in way to break lines within a cell.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{booktabs}

\begin{document}

\begin{tabular}{|p{5cm}|}
\toprule
Text line 1 \\
Text line 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\end{document}
```

Here's a simple function in C# to find duplicates in a collection. This function uses LINQ (Language Integrated Query) to group the elements by their values and then checks for groups with more than one element.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void FindDuplicates<T>(IEnumerable<T> collection)
    {
        var duplicates = collection.GroupBy(x => x)
                                   .Where(g => g.Count() > 1)
                                   .Select(g => new { Value = g.Key, Count = g.Count() });

        if (duplicates.Any())
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Duplicates found:");
            foreach (var duplicate in duplicates)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Value: {duplicate.Value}, Count: {duplicate.Count}");
            }
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("No duplicates found.");
        }
    }

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Example usage:
        var numbers = new int[] { 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 6 };
        FindDuplicates(numbers);

        var strings = new string[] { "apple", "banana", "apple", "orange", "banana" };
        FindDuplicates(strings);
    }
}
```

This function works as follows:

1.  `collection.GroupBy(x => x)`: Groups the elements of the collection by their values.
2.  `Where(g => g.Count() > 1)`: Filters the groups to only include those with more than one element (i.e., the duplicates).
3.  `Select(g => new { Value = g.Key, Count = g.Count() })`: Projects the groups into anonymous objects with properties for the value and count.
4.  The function then prints out the values and counts of the duplicates.

You can use this function with any type of collection (e.g., arrays, lists, sets) and it will find the duplicates for you.

Here is a VHDL UART transmitter design that meets your requirements:

```vhdl
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC;
use IEEE.NUMERIC_STD.ALL;

entity uart_transmitter is
    Port ( 
        clk : in STD_LOGIC;
        rst : in STD_LOGIC;
        data : in STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0);
        w : in STD_LOGIC;
        tx : out STD_LOGIC;
        baud_div : in STD_LOGIC_VECTOR (7 downto 0)
    );
end uart_transmitter;

architecture Behavioral of uart_transmitter is
    constant BAUD_RATE : integer := to_integer(unsigned(baud_div));
    signal cnt : integer range 0 to BAUD_RATE - 1 := 0;
    signal start : STD_LOGIC := '0';
begin
    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if rst = '1' then
                cnt <= 0;
                start <= '0';
            elsif w = '1' then
                start <= '1';
                cnt <= 0;
            elsif start = '1' then
                if cnt = BAUD_RATE - 1 then
                    start <= '0';
                    cnt <= 0;
                else
                    cnt <= cnt + 1;
                end if;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;

    process(clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            if start = '1' then
                if cnt < 8 then
                    tx <= data(cnt);
                    cnt <= cnt + 1;
                elsif cnt = 8 then
                    tx <= '0';
                    cnt <= cnt + 1;
                end if;
            end if;
        end if;
    end process;
end Behavioral;
```

Here is a brief explanation of the code:

- The entity declaration includes the necessary ports for the transmitter.
- The `BAUD_RATE` constant is calculated from the `baud_div` input.
- The `cnt` signal keeps track of the current baud rate count.
- The `start` signal is used to indicate the start of a transmission.
- In the first process, the `cnt` signal is incremented until it reaches the `BAUD_RATE - 1` value, at which point the transmission starts.
- In the second process, the `tx` signal is updated with the current data bit when the `start` signal is high and the `cnt` signal is less than 8.
- The `tx` signal remains low after the transmission is complete. 

This design assumes that the transmitter is reset when the `rst` input is high and that the transmission starts when a high pulse is written to the `w` input.

The issue you're experiencing is due to the fact that `sscanf` doesn't automatically remove the square brackets from the string. To fix this, you can use `strtok` to remove the square brackets from the string before passing it to `sscanf`. Here's how you can do it:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char str[] = "bleh [foo]";
    char arg[10];

    // Remove the square brackets from the string
    char *ptr = strtok(str, "[");
    ptr = strtok(NULL, "]");
    ptr = strtok(ptr, "]");

    // Now pass the string to sscanf
    if (sscanf(ptr, "%9s", arg) == 1) {
        printf("%s\n", arg);  // prints "foo"
    } else {
        printf("Error parsing string\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

However, a more C99 way to do this is to use `sscanf` with a format string that includes the `%n` modifier to get the number of characters read by `sscanf`, and then manually remove the square brackets from the string.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char str[] = "bleh [foo]";
    char arg[10];
    int len;

    if (sscanf(str, "bleh [%9s%n]", arg, &len) == 1) {
        // Remove the square brackets from the string
        if (str[len] == ']') {
            len++;
        }
        arg[len] = '\0';  // null-terminate the string

        printf("%s\n", arg);  // prints "foo"
    } else {
        printf("Error parsing string\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Both of these approaches will correctly remove the square brackets from the string before passing it to `printf`.

